    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 211740\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1040 AM MST Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil conditions, along with near to slightly above \n  normal temperatures, will continue for most of the region \n  through at least the middle portion of the week.\n\n- Low pressure and associated moisture will reintroduce rain \n  chances for portions of Arizona over the next few days, but any \n  rainfall activity for our forecast area will be focused \n  primarily over foothill and high terrain areas through \n  Wednesday.\n\n- Isolated lower desert rainfall chances get introduced to the\n  forecast during the latter portion of the week. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Wednesday/...\nCurrent objective analysis reveals shortwave troughing over the \nNorthern Plains, while a more potent upper-level low spins over \nCalifornia. The latter of these systems will be the main weather \nregime over the Desert Southwest during the front half of this week \nas it pushed intermediate low-amplitude ridging further eastward. \nHowever, the expectation is that the Pacific wave will weaken as it \nmoves further inland, so no significant day-to-day conditions \nchanges are in the forecast as dry and tranquil conditions will \ncontinue for the vast majority of the region. Even with modest \nheight falls, temperatures will continue to run near to slightly \nabove normal for this time of year, which translates to lower \nelevation highs hovering between 93-102 degrees.\n\nThe one exception to the dry and tranquil statement above during the \nfront half of the week will be portions of Northern and Eastern \nArizona thanks to increased moisture advection from the previously-\nmentioned Pacific low. Not only will decent moisture be in place \nover these areas with PWATs approaching 150-200% of normal, upper-\nlevel ascent from anti-cyclonic flow and an upper-level jet streak \nwill provide and added layer of support for the development of \nshower and thunderstorm activity. With that being said, coverage \nover our forecast area will be minimal at best as atmospheric \nthermodynamic profiles will not be quite enough to provide lower \ndesert rainfall. Rather, most, if not all, potential rainfall within \nour forecast boundaries will be confined to higher terrain areas. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Thursday through Sunday/... \nBy the latter half of the work week, the influence of the weakening \nsouthwestern low will all but disappear. The focus will then shift \ntoward the interplay between large scale troughing over the eastern \nPacific/West Coast, and the sub-tropical high over the Southern \nPlains and Northern Mexico. Global ensembles favor the retrograding \nback toward the Desert Southwest as we approach the weekend, likely \nstaving off any advance, at least temporarily, of more anti-cyclonic \nwaves. This progression would not completely scour residual moisture \nimmediately, so rain chances will likely linger into Thursday. \nCurrent PoPs show 30-50% chances for foothill and mountain areas and \n10-30% probabilities for the South-Central Arizona deserts. Beyond \nthat, persistent southwesterly flow would eventually dry us out \nenough to limit rain chances heading into the weekend. Another \ninflux of moisture by the end of the seven day period may bring \nrain chances back once again, but max chances at this time only \nrange between 20-30%. \n  \nEven with the competing airmasses, regional temperatures will only \nfluctuate within a few degrees through the weekend. While the ridge \nkeeps another shortwave from progressing toward the region, as \nmentioned previously, in return, the large scale troughing to our \nwest will keep the high from becoming too amplified, thankfully, \npreventing any further discussion of abnormal heat, at least during \nthis forecast period. Temperatures for the lower deserts Thursday \nthrough Sunday will range generally between 94-106 degrees, while \novernight lows hover near 70-82 degrees. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1740Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major aviation weather concerns will exist through Tuesday\nafternoon under periods of SCT-BKN mid and high cloud decks with\nbases remaining AOA 8K ft AGL. Winds will veer out of the SSE/S\nand maintain this direction through much of the afternoon,\npresenting a minor concern with a southerly cross-runway component\nat KPHX and KDVT. However, only occasional gusts to around 15 kts\nare expected through the afternoon with speeds remaining mostly\nAOB 10 kts. By this evening, a much later than usual westerly wind\nswitch is anticipated. A familiar pattern will take shape tonight\nand Tuesday morning, with E/SE nocturnal drainage winds\nestablishing followed by winds veering out of the SSE Tuesday\nmorning. Chances for SHRA/TSRA in the Phoenix airspace remain\nbelow 10% through Tuesday afternoon, with most activity remaining\nwell to the east and southeast, however, some periods with virga \nshowers in the vicinity cannot be ruled out. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nSHRA/TSRA will clear KBLH within the next hour. Outside of this\nactivity, no major impacts are anticipated through the next 24\nhours with periods of SCT mid and cloud decks becoming clear by\nthis evening. Winds will favor W/NW at KIPL, with a period of\ngusts to 20-25 kts expected this evening. At KBLH, winds will\nfavor S through the day with afternoon gusts between 15-20 kts\nlikely, followed by SW-W winds this evening into the overnight\nhours.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nNear to slightly above normal temperatures will prevail over the \nnext several days, with minimal day-to-day fluctuations. Dry \nconditions with no chances for rain continue through today ahead \nreturning high terrain rain chances for foothill and high terrain \nareas starting Tuesday. MinRHs between 15-30% will be common across \nthe region, with even higher readings (35-50%) for the far eastern \ndistricts. MaxRHs will range from fair to good over the western and \ncentral districts, to good and excellent for the eastern districts\ninto the weekend. Outside of some marginal breeziness caused by \nan approaching weather system, winds will follow familiar diurnal\ntrends with typical terrain induced afternoon gusts around 15-25 \nmph. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...RW\nLONG TERM...RW \nAVIATION...Whittock\nFIRE WEATHER...RW\n",
